      "writtenOn": "2020-11-16T19:27:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m wondering if maybe it would be best to not enter DP Alt Mode on the second port if one is already being used. What do you think?\n\nThis is messy and not ideal... 😞",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68fca288_ad18f9aa",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-16T20:00:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Trogdor SoC only supports one DP path. There are two approaches.\n\n(1) When a port enters DP alt-mode, it blocks the other port to enter DP alt-mode.\n(2) When a port receives HPD high, it ignores the other port HPD signals.\n\nIf doing (1), plugging a Type-C dongle with HDMI port, even no monitor connected, blocks the other port. It really confuses users. When they plug a monitor the other port, it doesn\u0027t work. They don\u0027t know the Type-C dongle blocks the DP path, even no monitor is connected.\n\nTrogdor does the (2) approach.",
      "parentUuid": "331ef705_83ca5df6",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2e29d7ea_8089f834",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125984
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-16T20:17:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah yeah, that is a really good point and further complicates things. \u003e\u003c\n\nI mean, I guess users will be confused if they try to use 2 displays anyways, but the case you describe is far worse.\n\nThat being said, I guess HPD is all we can go with. I _really_ don\u0027t like this, but what can we do... \n\nI\u0027m seeing a whole can of worms here. If a port is unplugged, I don\u0027t expect switching to automatically work to the other display. (Does it?) Wouldn\u0027t link training have to occur for the 2nd link?\n\nAlso, I assume that AUX is muxed as well? Or is it just the ML0-3 display lanes?\n\nAnyways, I will take a closer look today.",
      "parentUuid": "68fca288_ad18f9aa",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68dc2df6_fb2e6280",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-16T21:51:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "HPD is problematic because some dongles (WWCB approved Belkin F2CU038btBLK) flag HPD\u003dHigh, but utilize DP spec DPCD \"sinks present\u003d0\" register.\n\nThis spec-compliant optional behavior has caused problems before on this platform.\n\nAlso, many USB-C \"Data only\" docks actually claim to be DP sink endpoints since they recycle PD chipset code with \"fully populated\" video docks. We presented slides on this exact problem at USB DevDays 2019:\nhttps://usb.org/document-library/demystifying-usb-cr-using-linuxs-usb-type-cr-connector-class\n\nThus, with current suggested approaches, I fear a user with a \"data dock\" may inadvertently break display function on the other port, based on order of insertion.",
      "parentUuid": "2e29d7ea_8089f834",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0068a33c_fdf0603e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-16T22:10:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The http://go/cheza_typec doc has some explanation of this single-DP port design.\n\nI think the SoC should do a new link training when the HPD low pulse is longer than some IRQ interval. This low pulse will trigger a HPD low VDM message so the EC logic will switch the DP path.\n\nThere are a lot of other considerations on switching monitor. For example, two monitor plugged, when the first monitor connector becomes loosen that triggers HPD low, switching to the second monitor is not intended.\n\nSo I am still not convinced to implement switching the DP path based on HPD signal. It is still a TODO. Maybe the current no-op approach is better, that requires the user to unplug the dongle that disconnects the Type-C connection.\n\nThere are two external muxes, one for DP lanes and one for AUX.",
      "parentUuid": "68dc2df6_fb2e6280",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cdbb3767_67192d68",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125984
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-17T01:18:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the information. I think using HPD would be the most simple from the user\u0027s point of view; we could use a last recently used scheme. I *think* that should be okay? \n\nIf HPD were to go low for awhile, and we had HPD\u003d1 on the other port, maybe we could mux the other way and force the necessary low period to restart link training.",
      "parentUuid": "0068a33c_fdf0603e",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5064b522_bbeeb71c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-17T21:28:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If HPD were to go low for awhile, and we had HPD\u003d1 on the other port, maybe we could mux the other way and force the necessary low period to restart link training.\n\nI think proper fix is we need to pipe this information up to AP/GFX layer. DisplayPort layer (necessarily now, with Active Cables) needs to be transport/EC/PD-aware. Falsifying a HPD is a bit of a \"kludge\" due to our lacking this awareness. (Or logical DP state tracking per \"3.9.2 HPD Conversion\" in VESA DP over Type-C AltMode v2.0 which explicitly covers this scenario.)\n\nFor example: we currently do not honor HPD_WAIT from there. And we may be violating this requirement because we \"permanently tie logical HPD (PD) \u003c-\u003e physical HPD (GPIO)\".\n\n\u003e When DisplayPort Configuration is not selected and the converter is driving its HPD output\n\u003e low, the converter shall track HPD’s current state, ready for appropriatee indication when\n\u003e DisplayPort Configuration is subsequently selected.\n\nSo in order to be spec compliant, perhaps more difficult piping is needed. In the interest of time, merely documenting and moving on.",
      "parentUuid": "cdbb3767_67192d68",
      "revId": "33bf350c8f649f2702da17e289e8edd105db98ad",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
